---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '../../lib/site';
import { articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const siteUrl = (Astro.site ?? Astro.url).origin;
const path = '/guides/fundraising-in-india';

const howTo = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: 'How to raise early-stage funding for an Indian startup',
  description:
    'A practical guide for Indian founders: angel rounds, iSAFE notes, SISFS grants, dilution math, valuation, and term sheets.',
  inLanguage: site.language,
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: 'Validate traction before pitching', text: 'Have clear proof of demand, an early prototype, or customer waitlists before reaching out to angels or syndicates.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: 'Apply for government non-dilutive grants', text: 'Secure DPIIT recognition and apply to incubators for the ₹50L Startup India Seed Fund Scheme (SISFS).' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: 'Choose the right investment instrument', text: 'Use iSAFE notes (CCPS-based) for pre-seed angel cheques to avoid premature valuation battles.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 4, name: 'Structure the cap table and control dilution', text: 'Do not dilute more than 10-15% in pre-seed and 15-20% in seed to protect founder ownership for future rounds.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 5, name: 'Close the round and complete ROC & FEMA filings', text: 'Pass board & shareholder resolutions, file PAS-3 with ROC, and file FC-GPR on the RBI FIRMS portal if foreign capital is involved.' },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title="Early-Stage Startup Fundraising Guide India (2026) — Angels, iSAFE, Grants & VCs"
  description="The practical playbook for Indian founders: raising angel rounds, using iSAFE notes, applying for the ₹50L SISFS grant, cap table dilution, and term sheet red flags."
  type="article"
  keywords={[
    'startup fundraising India',
    'iSAFE note India',
    'how to raise seed round India',
    'Startup India Seed Fund Scheme SISFS',
    'angel investment India',
    'cap table dilution startup India',
    'CCPS vs equity India',
  ]}
  jsonLd={[
    breadcrumbSchema(siteUrl, [
      { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
      { name: 'Guides', path: '/guides' },
      { name: 'Early-Stage Fundraising Guide', path: path },
    ]),
    articleSchema({
      siteUrl,
      title: 'Early-Stage Startup Fundraising Guide India (2026)',
      description: 'The practical playbook for Indian founders: angel rounds, iSAFE notes, SISFS grants, dilution math, and term sheets.',
      path,
      type: 'TechArticle',
    }),
    howTo,
  ]}
>
  <article class="container guide">
    <p class="eyebrow">Founder Guide · Updated 2026</p>
    <h1>Early-stage fundraising in India: from first cheque to term sheet</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Raising outside capital is not validation of a product — it is selling part of your company to buy speed.
      This guide covers how early-stage Indian startups actually raise: from friends & family and angel syndicates to iSAFE notes, government grants, and institutional seed rounds.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#stages">1. Early funding stages: who writes cheques when?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#instruments">2. Investment instruments: iSAFE vs CCPS vs Equity</a></li>
        <li><a href="#grants">3. Non-dilutive capital: Startup India Seed Fund (SISFS)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dilution">4. Valuation, dilution math & cap table hygiene</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms">5. Term sheet red flags to watch out for</a></li>
        <li><a href="#legal">6. Post-round compliance: ROC & FEMA (FC-GPR)</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section id="stages">
      <h2>1. Early funding stages: who writes cheques when?</h2>
      <p>
        In India, early-stage capital typically breaks down into three micro-phases before you talk to large venture funds:
      </p>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Pre-seed / Friends, Family & Angels (₹15 Lakhs – ₹1 Crore):</strong>
          Written by high-net-worth individuals (HNIs), operators, or angel networks (e.g. Inflection Point Ventures, Indian Angel Network, angels on platforms like LetsVenture or AngelList India). At this point, you usually have a prototype, an MVP, or early signals of founder-market fit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Micro-VCs & Seed Accelerators (₹1 Crore – ₹4 Crores):</strong>
          Funds like 100X.VC, India Quotient, Antler India, or early-stage syndicates. They look for early pilot users, retention, or waitlist metrics and clear market size.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Institutional Seed / Pre-Series A ($500k – $2M+):</strong>
          Traditional seed funds (Peak XV Surge, Elevation Capital, Lightspeed, Blume Ventures). Requires proven unit economics, repeatable customer acquisition, and a defensible moat.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="instruments">
      <h2>2. Investment instruments: iSAFE vs CCPS vs Equity</h2>
      <p>
        You should almost never issue straight equity shares (common equity) in an early angel round because assigning an exact valuation when you have little revenue leads to endless negotiation. Indian founders use:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="comparison-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Instrument</th>
              <th>How it works in India</th>
              <th>Best used for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>iSAFE Note</strong></td>
              <td>
                India Simple Agreement for Future Equity (modeled as a convertible security with 0.0001% CCPS). Postpones valuation until the next priced round with a valuation cap or discount.
              </td>
              <td>Pre-seed rounds, angel cheques, fast closings without high legal bills.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>CCPS (Compulsorily Convertible Preference Shares)</strong></td>
              <td>
                Standard equity-like preference shares that convert to ordinary equity upon an IPO, liquidation, or subsequent funding. Has liquidation preference.
              </td>
              <td>Priced institutional seed rounds and Series A rounds.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Convertible Notes</strong></td>
              <td>
                Short-term debt instrument that converts to equity. Under Indian Companies Act, only <strong>DPIIT-recognised startups</strong> can issue convertible notes for up to 10 years with a minimum ticket size of ₹25 Lakhs per investor.
              </td>
              <td>Bridge rounds between seed and Series A.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="grants">
      <h2>3. Non-dilutive capital: Startup India Seed Fund (SISFS)</h2>
      <p>
        Before you give away equity, check if you qualify for government grants. The flagship scheme is the <strong>Startup India Seed Fund Scheme (SISFS)</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Up to ₹20 Lakhs as a 100% grant</strong> for proof of concept, prototype development, or product trials.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Up to ₹50 Lakhs as debt or convertible debentures</strong> for market-entry, commercialisation, and scaling.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How to apply:</strong> You do not apply directly to a government ministry. You get DPIIT recognition first, then submit an application via the Startup India portal selecting up to 3 approved incubators (e.g. IIT/IIM incubators, T-Hub, etc.).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Explore our deep-dive FAQ: <a href="/faq/sisfs">What is the Startup India Seed Fund Scheme?</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="dilution">
      <h2>4. Valuation, dilution math & cap table hygiene</h2>
      <p>
        A frequent early-stage mistake is giving away 30% to 40% of the company to an angel or incubator in the first ₹50 Lakhs round. That makes future institutional rounds nearly uninvestable.
      </p>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Pre-seed dilution rule:</strong> Aim to sell no more than <strong>10% to 15%</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Seed round dilution rule:</strong> Typically <strong>15% to 20%</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Founder pool retention:</strong> Ensure the co-founders collectively own at least <strong>55% to 65%</strong> going into Series A. If you are already diluted below 40% before product-market fit, institutional VCs will hesitate because the founders lack long-term upside.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>ESOP Pool:</strong> Investors will often ask you to create a 10% unallocated ESOP pool <em>before</em> their money comes in ("pre-money pool"), which dilutes only the founders. Negotiate to keep the pool sized to realistic hiring needs for the next 18 months (e.g. 5–7%) rather than a blanket 15%.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="terms">
      <h2>5. Term sheet red flags to watch out for</h2>
      <p>
        When you receive an angel or VC term sheet, valuation is only one line. Look out for these terms:
      </p>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Participating Liquidation Preference:</strong> Always insist on <em>1x Non-Participating</em> preference. A "participating" preference means the investor gets their money back first AND also shares in the remaining proceeds as an equity holder — very unfavorable to founders.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Excessive Board Control:</strong> In seed rounds, early investors should not control board majority. Standard is a 3-member board: 2 founders + 1 investor representative (or 2 founders + 0 investor board seats with an observer seat).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Harsh Founder Vesting & Bad Leaver Clauses:</strong> Investors will require founders to vest their shares over 4 years with a 1-year cliff. Ensure "bad leaver" is strictly defined as fraud, gross negligence, or criminal acts — not just disagreeing on strategy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Exclusivity / No-Shop Period:</strong> Standard is 30 to 45 days. Do not agree to 90+ days of exclusivity without a committed term sheet and legal escrow in sight.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="legal">
      <h2>6. Post-round compliance: ROC & FEMA (FC-GPR)</h2>
      <p>
        Once the cheques clear into your company bank account, the legal work begins:
      </p>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Board & Shareholder Approvals:</strong> Convene an EGM (Extraordinary General Meeting) to approve the private placement offer (PAS-4).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>ROC Filing (Form PAS-3):</strong> Within 30 days of share allotment, file Form PAS-3 on the MCA portal along with the valuation report and list of allottees.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Foreign Capital / FDI Compliance (FC-GPR):</strong> If an overseas investor (e.g., US angel, Y Combinator, foreign VC) sends funds, you must obtain a FIRC (Foreign Inward Remittance Certificate) and KYC from your bank and file Form FC-GPR on the RBI FIRMS portal within 30 days of allotment. Failing to file triggers severe FEMA penalties.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Angel Tax is Abolished:</strong> As of the 2024 Union Budget, Section 56(2)(viib) ("Angel Tax") is abolished for both resident and non-resident investments into unlisted companies. You still need an independent valuation report for FEMA and auditor records, but the punitive tax penalty is gone.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="next-step glass">
      <h3>Ready to incorporate or prepare your company for investment?</h3>
      <p>
        Read our step-by-step <a href="/guides/incorporate-in-india">Incorporation Guide</a> to set up a clean Pvt Ltd, or explore specific answers in the <a href="/faq">Founder FAQ</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="disclaimer">
      <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This guide is for educational purposes for startup founders and does not constitute formal legal or financial advice. Consult a qualified Company Secretary (CS) and corporate lawyer when drafting investment agreements.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>

